---
import BaseLayout from "zuedocs/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { primaryNav, siteConfig } from "../data/docs";
import "../styles/landing.css";

const productIdeas = [
  {
    title: "Discover",
    body: "Search public skills, inspect authors and releases, follow people you trust, and subscribe without creating an account.",
    href: "/docs/guides/discover-and-subscribe"
  },
  {
    title: "Publish",
    body: "Bring an existing skill under Denju, keep private revision history across devices, and publish immutable releases only when you choose.",
    href: "/docs/guides/publish-and-edit"
  },
  {
    title: "Compose",
    body: "A pack turns several skills into one versioned set. Let a skill follow latest when you want updates, or pin an exact release when you need stability.",
    href: "/docs/guides/packs"
  },
  {
    title: "Standardize",
    body: "Assign a pack to a team and it becomes policy: current and future members continuously receive the same approved skill set.",
    href: "/docs/guides/teams"
  }
];

const architecturePoints = [
  ["Only what changed", "Merkle trees reuse unchanged blobs and subtrees, so editing one file does not turn the whole skill into a new transfer."],
  ["Current state, not event replay", "After being offline, a client compares the refs it knows with the registry and jumps directly to what it should have now."],
  ["Complete before visible", "Remote updates are verified and assembled off-path, then switched into view atomically. Agents never see a half-updated skill."],
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${siteConfig.name} | ${siteConfig.strapline}`}
  description={siteConfig.description}
  siteConfig={siteConfig}
  primaryNav={primaryNav}
>
  <main class="denju-landing">
    <section class="hero shell">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Agent Skills, synchronized</p>
        <h1>The skills your agents use, kept current.</h1>
        <p class="hero-lede">
          Denju is a registry and synchronization system for Agent Skills. Find a skill,
          subscribe once, and Denju keeps the right version available to Codex and Claude Code
          on every machine—without copying folders around by hand.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button button-primary" href="/docs/start/install">Get started</a>
          <a class="button button-secondary" href="/docs/start/what-is-denju">How Denju works</a>
        </div>
        <p class="hero-note">Public search and subscriptions work without an account.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="subscription-demo" aria-label="A Denju subscription following the latest skill release across devices">
        <div class="demo-card demo-skill">
          <div class="demo-card-head">
            <div>
              <span class="demo-kicker">public skill</span>
              <strong>@ashray/react-performance</strong>
            </div>
            <span class="star-count">★ 482</span>
          </div>
          <p>Patterns for profiling and improving React application performance.</p>
          <div class="release-row">
            <span>latest</span>
            <strong>v13</strong>
            <i></i>
            <span>published 8m ago</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="relationship">
          <span class="relationship-label">your subscription</span>
          <code>@ashray/react-performance → latest</code>
          <span class="relationship-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </div>

        <div class="device-list">
          <div class="device-row">
            <span class="device-icon">M</span>
            <div><strong>MacBook</strong><small>Codex · Claude Code</small></div>
            <span class="device-version">v13</span>
            <span class="sync-state">synced</span>
          </div>
          <div class="device-row">
            <span class="device-icon">W</span>
            <div><strong>Workstation</strong><small>Codex</small></div>
            <span class="device-version">v13</span>
            <span class="sync-state">synced</span>
          </div>
          <div class="device-row device-row-muted">
            <span class="device-icon">L</span>
            <div><strong>Offline laptop</strong><small>last seen yesterday</small></div>
            <span class="device-version">v12</span>
            <span class="sync-state">catches up on reconnect</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="problem shell">
      <div class="section-label">Why Denju exists</div>
      <div class="problem-grid">
        <h2>A skill is easy to copy.<br />Keeping copies true is the hard part.</h2>
        <div class="problem-copy">
          <p>
            Agent Skills are ordinary directories. That simplicity is a strength—but once the same skill
            lives on two machines, in two harnesses, or across a team, folders start to drift.
          </p>
          <p>
            Denju moves the unit of management up one level. Instead of remembering where you copied a
            folder, you subscribe to the skill, pack, or team policy you want. Denju keeps the right folders
            and versions on each machine automatically.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="before-after" aria-label="The difference between copying skill folders and subscribing with Denju">
        <div class="before-after-column before">
          <span class="comparison-label">copying folders</span>
          <div class="copy-row"><code>review/</code><span>MacBook</span><b>v4</b></div>
          <div class="copy-row"><code>review/</code><span>Workstation</span><b>v3</b></div>
          <div class="copy-row"><code>review-copy/</code><span>teammate</span><b>?</b></div>
          <p>Which copy is canonical? Who has the update? What happens after an agent edits one?</p>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
        <div class="before-after-column after">
          <span class="comparison-label">one Denju relationship</span>
          <div class="subscription-pill"><code>@alice/review</code><span>latest</span></div>
          <div class="desired-state-row"><span>MacBook</span><b>v4</b><em>current</em></div>
          <div class="desired-state-row"><span>Workstation</span><b>v4</b><em>current</em></div>
          <div class="desired-state-row"><span>teammate</span><b>v4</b><em>current</em></div>
          <p>The subscription is what you keep. Denju keeps the local folders current for you.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="model shell" id="model">
      <div class="section-label">The model</div>
      <div class="model-intro">
        <h2>One small vocabulary covers the whole product.</h2>
        <p>
          Denju deliberately avoids turning skills into Git repositories or dependency graphs.
          A few concepts are enough to describe how people actually want skills to move.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="model-rows">
        <article class="model-row">
          <div class="model-term">Skill</div>
          <div class="model-example"><code>@alice/review</code><span>v1</span><span>v2</span><span class="active">v3</span></div>
          <p>A complete Agent Skill directory with immutable releases and a moving <code>latest</code>.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="model-row">
          <div class="model-term">Subscription</div>
          <div class="model-example"><code>@alice/review</code><span class="operator">→</span><span class="active">latest</span></div>
          <p>A simple promise: keep this skill installed. Follow latest by default, or pin one exact release.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="model-row">
          <div class="model-term">Pack</div>
          <div class="model-example model-example-pack"><span>review · latest</span><span>testing · v3</span><span>security · latest</span></div>
          <p>A flat, versioned set of skills. One subscription can keep an entire working setup current.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="model-row">
          <div class="model-term">Team</div>
          <div class="model-example"><code>@acme/packs/core</code><span class="operator">→</span><span class="active">all members</span></div>
          <p>A shared home for organization skills and packs. Assign a pack and its skills stay installed for current and future team members.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="product shell">
      <div class="section-label">What you can do</div>
      <div class="product-heading">
        <h2>Start as a consumer.<br />Grow into a registry for your team.</h2>
        <p>
          Identity and collaboration are lazy. You can use the public registry anonymously, then claim
          a username only when you have something to publish, share, or manage with a team.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="product-list">
        {productIdeas.map((item, index) => (
          <a class="product-item" href={item.href}>
            <span class="product-index">0{index + 1}</span>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p>{item.body}</p>
            <span class="product-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="team-story shell">
      <div class="team-copy">
        <div class="section-label">The team use case</div>
        <h2>Make your best practices self-installing.</h2>
        <p>
          Put approved skills into a pack and assign it to a team. Join the team on a new machine and
          Denju installs those skills automatically. Change the pack later and everyone stays up to date.
        </p>
        <a class="inline-link" href="/docs/guides/teams">Teams and assigned packs →</a>
      </div>

      <div class="team-visual" aria-label="A team pack automatically applied to team members">
        <div class="pack-card">
          <div class="pack-title"><span>@acme/packs/core</span><strong>v18</strong></div>
          <div><span>code-review</span><b>latest</b></div>
          <div><span>testing</span><b>v3</b></div>
          <div><span>security</span><b>latest</b></div>
        </div>
        <div class="pack-assignment"><span>assigned to @acme</span><i>→</i></div>
        <div class="member-grid">
          <div><span>A</span><small>3/3 skills</small></div>
          <div><span>B</span><small>3/3 skills</small></div>
          <div><span>C</span><small>3/3 skills</small></div>
          <div><span>new</span><small>syncs on join</small></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="collaboration shell">
      <div class="section-label">Safe to edit</div>
      <div class="collaboration-grid">
        <div>
          <h2>Subscribed does not mean read-only.</h2>
          <p>
            If you edit somebody else's subscribed skill, Denju protects the change by turning it into
            your fork. The upstream subscription is never silently mutated or overwritten.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="collab-points">
          <div><strong>Fork automatically</strong><span>Your edit becomes independent work with immutable upstream provenance.</span></div>
          <div><strong>Sync upstream explicitly</strong><span>Clean changes merge automatically; real conflicts preserve both sides.</span></div>
          <div><strong>Propose the result</strong><span>Send the current fork head upstream without inventing branches, pull requests, or a review inbox.</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="architecture shell">
      <div class="architecture-head">
        <div>
          <div class="section-label">Under the hood</div>
          <h2>Efficient because Denju knows what changed—not just that a folder changed.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          You never need to understand the storage model to use Denju. But the same design that makes
          history precise also keeps synchronization small, fast, and recoverable.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="architecture-points">
        {architecturePoints.map(([title, body]) => (
          <article><h3>{title}</h3><p>{body}</p></article>
        ))}
      </div>
      <a class="inline-link" href="/docs/architecture/overview">Read the architecture →</a>
    </section>

    <section class="self-host shell">
      <div class="self-host-card">
        <div>
          <div class="section-label">Your registry, if you want it</div>
          <h2>The hosted service and self-hosted Denju are the same product.</h2>
          <p>
            The official registry is the default. Organizations can run the same open-source
            <code>denju-server</code> against PostgreSQL and S3-compatible storage. The reference Compose
            stack includes PostgreSQL and Garage; managed services work too.
          </p>
          <div class="self-host-actions">
            <a class="button button-primary" href="/docs/self-host/quickstart">Self-host Denju</a>
            <a class="button button-secondary" href="https://github.com/amxv/denju" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View source</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="infra-stack" aria-label="Denju server backed by PostgreSQL and S3-compatible storage">
          <div class="infra-main"><span>denju-server</span><small>same container · your origin</small></div>
          <div class="infra-children">
            <div><strong>PostgreSQL</strong><small>refs · identity · access · policy</small></div>
            <div><strong>S3 compatible</strong><small>immutable skill content</small></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="quickstart shell">
      <div class="quickstart-card">
        <div class="quickstart-copy">
          <div class="section-label">Start here</div>
          <h2>Your first subscription is four commands.</h2>
          <p>Setup and public use are anonymous. Claim an identity later, when you actually need one.</p>
        </div>
        <pre><code><span>$</span> denju setup
<span>$</span> denju search "react performance"
<span>$</span> denju show @ashray/react-performance
<span>$</span> denju subscribe @ashray/react-performance</code></pre>
        <div class="quickstart-actions">
          <a class="button button-inverse" href="/docs/start/install">Install Denju</a>
          <a class="text-link-inverse" href="/docs">Browse documentation →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
